;; cppi's divergences from g++ 15.2 (doc/cpp/CPP-SURVEY.md, "As built: CX8 (write-ahead)"; doc/cpp/LLVM.md §11): the classes every
;; program of bin/cppi check that does not pass falls into, each with why and where it was decided, in test/goi/divergences.edn's
;; format. A program's class is the first whose :match finds its result and reason ("RESULT REASON", as
;; test/cpp/check-linux-amd64.edn writes them). bin/cppi divergences judges the recorded check against them. Read with the
;; Clojure reader.
[
 {:id :threads
  :title "threads (deferred: ci runs no threads)"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "std::thread starts a thread by musl's pthread_create, whose clone is assembly: ci's back end has no threads (C's class threads, doc/c/SPEC.md §6.5); CP1 left threads out of v1"
  :decided "CP1; C's §6.5, CS264 (a)"
  :match [#"^fail differ status 139 .*undefined function __clone"]}
 {:id :long-double
  :title "the IR's x86_fp80 layout against ci's 8-byte long double (CS35, CP84 (a))"
  :kind :design
  :why "the lowering keeps the IR's 16-byte x86_fp80 in its structs' static assertions (CP84 (a)); ci's long double is double (CS35), so a program or a runtime unit it links whose types hold one (std::format's argument, numeric_limits<long double> users) fails ci's check, and one that computes in long double differs from g++'s x87"
  :decided "CS35, CP84 (a)"
  ;; only the failures CS35 and CP84 (a) explain (review 191, F1): the static assertions of a layout holding an x86_fp80 (the
  ;; program's, or a runtime unit's it links), and C's two differ shapes of a computation in long double
  :match [#"^check-error (\d+: static assertion failed(; )?)+ uses-long-double$"
          #"^refused runtime-check: .*static assertion failed.* uses-long-double$"
          #"^fail differ (status 134 \(g\+\+ 0\)|stdout) uses-long-double$"]}
 {:id :ci-compound-literal
  :title "ci's address of a scalar compound literal (a ci gap, C's to fix)"
  :kind :gap
  :why "ci's builder evaluates &(T){v} of a scalar type to v (bin/ci run: memcpy(&x, &(unsigned){7u}, 4) faults at 0x7); the lowering writes it for odd-width integer stores (i24, i40, i48: bit-field access units) and some bitcasts"
  :decided "CX8 (this record); reported to C's crop"
  :match [#"^fail known ci-compound-literal: "]}
 {:id :speed
  :title "speed: programs beyond the check's budget"
  :kind :design
  :why "an interpreter of nodes is 10 to 100 times slower than g++'s code (C's and goi's class of the same name)"
  :decided "C's CS107 (a); GOI-SPEC §11.1's rule"
  :match [#"^fail differ status timeout"]}
 {:id :oracle-text
  :title "text the compiler writes into the program: the oracle's, not g++'s"
  :kind :design
  :why "__PRETTY_FUNCTION__, __FUNCTION__'s decorations and similar compiler-made strings come from clang, which the lowered program is compiled by (CP11's split: g++ judges, clang compiles); a test that compares them with g++'s spelling differs"
  :decided "CP2, CP11"
  :match [#"^fail known oracle-text: "]}
 {:id :musl-unread
  :title "a musl source ci's forms cache does not hold (C's gap)"
  :kind :gap
  :why "five of musl 1.2.6's sources are not read by repr/c (split-declaration, C3b's class: src/errno/strerror.c among them), so a program that reaches strerror (std::error_category::message, std::filesystem's errors) faults on the undefined function; C's to read"
  :decided "C's C5 (bin/ci musl read's \"not read\" list); CX8 (this record)"
  :match [#"^fail differ status 139 .*undefined function (strerror|iswpunct|wcwidth|iconv)\b"]}
 {:id :atomic-generic
  :title "an atomic of a floating type (deferred)"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "the lowering writes gcc's generic __atomic_load, __atomic_store and __atomic_exchange for a float or double atomic (LLVM.md §7); repr/c's <builtin> source declares them without parameters, so ci's check refuses the calls (\"too many arguments\"); the lowering could use the _n forms on the integer of the same size"
  :decided "CX8 (this record)"
  ;; flagged by the program's own calls (review 191, F3): ci's message names no function
  :match [#"^check-error .*too many arguments to function.* uses-atomic-generic( uses-long-double)?$"]}
 {:id :va-arg
  :title "a lowered function that reads its va_list (a gap: ci's x86-64 va_list later)"
  :kind :gap
  :why "clang writes va_arg for x86-64 at -O0 inline, reading the __va_list_tag's gp_offset and reg_save_area; on ci the storage holds ci's own va_list, so the function reads garbage and faults (queue 191's va3.cpp: a vsum(int, va_list) with va_arg faults at 0x28). The lowering marks the program's functions whose IR does so (va-arg=NAME in the build's flags) and the judge classes a fault in one of them. Passing a va_list on (vsnprintf, va_copy, a lowered forwarder) works; libstdc++'s one such function, __snprintf_lite, has ci's override (test/cpp/runtime/ci/)"
  :decided "the user, 2026-11-03 (queue 191's DECISION (a)): a class now; ci's x86-64 va_list with C++'s next round"
  :match [#"^fail differ status 139 .* uses-va-arg( uses-long-double)?$"]}
 {:id :oracle-rejected
  :title "clang rejects a program g++ builds"
  :kind :design
  :why "the oracle (clang 23) refuses a GNU extension or a g++-only construct the test uses; the program has no IR to lower (CP11: g++ the judge, clang the oracle)"
  :decided "CP2, CP11"
  :match [#"^oracle-rejected "]}
]
